WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.760
My name's Heather Browning, and I'm here on behalf of my daughter, Bronwyn,

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.300
who has multiple myeloma. I'm accompanied by Barbara and

3
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.940
Nicola from Myeloma New Zealand, who'll speak to the broader

4
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.180
issues and concerns for people across Aotearoa with

5
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.080
myeloma. I'll speak very specifically to the situation for

6
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:20.740
my whanau.

7
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.310
I just want to give you some context to our situation.

8
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.220
In 1999, I was diagnosed with Stage 2B cervical

9
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.960
cancer. It was a large tumor, it'd gone undetected despite

10
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.400
regular pap smears, et cetera.

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.780
My daughter, Bronwyn, my only child, was just 10 years old,

12
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.560
and I was solo parenting at the time.

13
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.340
And at that time, things looked pretty grim,

14
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.880
and through the following year of radiotherapy and

15
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.480
surgeries. Clearly, I've survived,

16
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.880
and also prevailed through two episodes of melanoma.

17
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.320
Nonetheless, those years were pretty tough for my daughter.

18
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.940
Fast-forward to 2017, when the first of my three brothers

19
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.431
succumbed to metastatic lung cancer.

20
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.960
In May 2018, the second of my brothers died, also from lung

21
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.440
cancer. And just over one year later, in June

22
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.700
2019, my remaining brother died from

23
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.720
invasive colorectal cancer. Again,

24
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.880
those were dark days for my daughter and me.

25
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.290
My three brothers, Bronwyn's beloved uncles, were all gone within two

26
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.300
years of one another.

27
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.820
So we moved on, and just when we thought we could really move on, and after

28
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:34.440
COVID,

29
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.740
in February 2022, Bronwyn

30
00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.800
was diagnosed with multiple myeloma.

31
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.520
She was 33 years old.

32
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.280
Apparently very young to be receiving such a diagnosis, a fact that

33
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.760
was tediously noted to us by every health professional she

34
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.400
encountered.

35
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.220
So to say that we were shocked

36
00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.560
or distraught or overwhelmed by this news is a massive

37
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:01.400
understatement.

38
00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.760
The cancer legacy, it's terrible.

39
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.780
The cancer legacy in our whanau is very confronting

40
00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:12.640
and something that Bronwyn has lived with throughout her life.

41
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.860
But there's a stark difference between the nature of cancers and the

42
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.600
available treatments of my brothers and me versus the

43
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:22.860
situation for Bronwyn.

44
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.220
We all had preventable or avoidable cancers

45
00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.540
if we'd known at the time, at crucial stages in our lives, what to avoid

46
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.000
or have had access to

47
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.640
screening that's now available.

48
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.080
And once diagnosed, we all had a range of options for treatment,

49
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.100
including radiotherapy, chemotherapy, and surgeries.

50
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.500
That's how it is with tumor cancers,

51
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.329
but none of that is how it is with blood cancers, with

52
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.730
myeloma. Myeloma has no known cause,

53
00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.329
so there's no mechanisms for prevention.

54
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:04.000
There is no screening program, and diagnoses are most

55
00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.820
often arrived at incidentally when a person presents

56
00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.940
with some other vague ailment or pain.

57
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.860
But the greater jeopardy with myeloma

58
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.560
is that the treatment options in Aotearoa are extremely limited

59
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.260
and are way out of kilter with other OECD countries.

60
00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.980
Stem cell transplants are not curative.

61
00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:29.600
They simply delay the inevitable,

62
00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.420
and the maintenance options are limited.

63
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.580
Throughout 2022, Bronwyn went through the necessary processes of

64
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.620
chemotherapy prior to an autologous stem cell transplant

65
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.420
in November 2022.

66
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.500
This pushed her into remission, thankfully, and to date, she remains in

67
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.040
remission,

68
00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.730
but on a maintenance program, a regime of

69
00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.280
lenalidomide.

70
00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:00.100
As a result of the initial chemotherapy, Bronwyn is

71
00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.440
now unable to have children,

72
00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:05.540
and that's been a very high price for her and her husband.

73
00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.970
Nonetheless, they've designed a good life for themselves, and she's

74
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.360
choosing as much as she is able not to be defined by

75
00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:20.180
myeloma. She's currently studying for her

76
00:04:20.221 --> 00:04:24.130
master's in zoology, which is where her passion is and where she focuses

77
00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:24.820
her energy.

78
00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.310
Hence me talking to you. She's using

79
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.660
lenalidomide to hold myeloma at bay, but it's a

80
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:33.820
double-edged sword.

81
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.040
While it is a lifeline of sorts, it's a terrible drug with an array of awful

82
00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:43.000
side effects that impact daily on her quality of life and wellbeing.

83
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.720
To add to those challenges, the long-term use of lenalidomide can

84
00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:51.380
create new risks for other cancers, but Bron has no

85
00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.980
choice. She's no other options here in Aotearoa.

86
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.660
Bron knows that she will not remain in remission

87
00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.980
indefinitely. She knows it's just a matter of time.

88
00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.660
It's an ever-present shadow for her.

89
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.690
She's hoping, well, I guess we're all hoping,

90
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.380
that her remission will last long enough that other options for treatment become

91
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.070
available. Things like immunotherapies,

92
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.910
CAR T-cell therapy, et cetera. All those options that are currently

93
00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.690
available in many other countries.

94
00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:24.760
We want those for her in Aotearoa.

95
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.220
I want my daughter to have more than hope.

96
00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.910
Hope isn't enough. We've been there, done that. Hope doesn't work.

97
00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.800
Hope alone is an injustice. It's an energy thief

98
00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:41.340
if there's not something tangible and meaningful to hope for.

99
00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:46.380
So we need certainty that in the very near future, Bronwyn

100
00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:50.290
and all the others in a situation like hers will have

101
00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.580
treatment options that give the opportunity for her to have a full,

102
00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.760
long, and enjoyable life.

103
00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.240
That's me. Thank you.

104
00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:00.560
I'll pass over to Barbara

105
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:01.920
Thanks, Heather.

106
00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.780
Kia ora and good morning. I'm Barbara Horn, chair of Myeloma New

107
00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.540
Zealand, and joined today by Nicola Oakenvill.

108
00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:14.090
Thank you for the opportunity to speak with you about only three, we're

109
00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.600
only going to choose three, of the issues facing New Zealand's myeloma

110
00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.900
community and what can be done about them.

111
00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.560
Some background, more than 3,500 New Zealanders are living with

112
00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.420
myeloma, with over 450 newly diagnosed each year.

113
00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.500
While the average age at diagnosis is between 65 and 70,

114
00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.920
New Zealand patients range in age from their 20s.

115
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.790
Māori and Pasifika experience higher diagnosis rates, they are

116
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.760
diagnosed younger, and they have lower survival rates.

117
00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.760
Myeloma is a relentless cancer of the plasma cells found in

118
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.880
bone marrow, and it's a relapsing-remitting cancer.

119
00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.080
So that means it can respond to treatment and go into remission, and then it

120
00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.200
will come back. Imagine how this plays out.

121
00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.410
You get diagnosed with something you've probably never heard of.

122
00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:04.590
There's a line of treatment that might or might not work.

123
00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:08.990
If it fails or causes debilitating side effects, or if it works for

124
00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:12.960
a while and then you relapse, as you will, you can't keep using the same

125
00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.550
treatment, so there is another line, and another line, then another.

126
00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.300
But not in New Zealand.

127
00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:22.860
That's the way it is in countries where access to medicines makes myeloma a

128
00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.980
chronic but manageable disease. But not here.

129
00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.980
And that's issue number one, access to modern medicines and

130
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.580
treatments.

131
00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.980
In New Zealand, you are diagnosed, you begin the regime of weekly hospital

132
00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.960
visits and learn this new complex language of myeloma, and you and your

133
00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.820
people work out how to manage this new normal, and the frustration

134
00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.960
begins of feeling there is very little anyone can do to help.

135
00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.020
New Zealand treatment options for myeloma are very limited, and they include

136
00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:53.980
thalidomide, a 70-year-old medicine with some history.

137
00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:58.400
While it can be a viable medicine to use, it and the other medicines readily

138
00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.760
available and funded here are generally old, less

139
00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.660
effective, and less patient-friendly than modern medicines.

140
00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.020
Newer treatments are easier to tolerate, they provide effective treatment for

141
00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:12.980
longer, and allow patients to live a more normal life with

142
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.900
longer times between the inevitable relapses.

143
00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:21.290
The issue of access to modern medicines also has an implication for

144
00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:25.380
access to clinical trials. These are an essential tool for clinicians and

145
00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.080
patients as a way to access newer medicines.

146
00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:32.600
The reality is, New Zealand hematologists often cannot accept trials

147
00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.160
because daratumumab is not available here.

148
00:08:35.841 --> 00:08:39.740
This standard of care medicine, funded in more than 50 countries, is often a

149
00:08:39.821 --> 00:08:43.520
comparator or a prerequisite treatment for trial participation.

150
00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.580
Issue two is that funding just one more medicine will not be a silver bullet.

151
00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.900
Myeloma is highly individual, so clinicians need a range of medicines

152
00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.620
they can combine and sequence for each patient.

153
00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:00.190
To do this, Australia funds five more myeloma treatments than New

154
00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:03.620
Zealand, and the UK funds nine more.

155
00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:08.840
The reality of these two issues is that New Zealanders with myeloma die earlier

156
00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.860
than they need to. Those who can pay for unfunded treatments

157
00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.160
often take drastic options to fund treatment privately in New Zealand or

158
00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:17.760
overseas.

159
00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:22.900
And issue three then is that even private health insurance may not

160
00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:23.740
provide access.

161
00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.340
Although a patient may have sufficient cover for non-PharmAC-funded

162
00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.560
medicines, that cover is likely to apply

163
00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.740
only to Medsafe-registered medicines.

164
00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:39.220
The currently low likelihood of new treatments being funded by PharmAC

165
00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:43.360
can discourage pharmaceutical companies from registering these modern

166
00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.220
life-prolonging myeloma medicines in New Zealand.

167
00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.960
This means a myeloma patient in New Zealand with private medical insurance

168
00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.960
will likely have access to fewer medicines than a publicly treated

169
00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.480
patient in Australia.

170
00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:01.360
To be clear, we're not suggesting the solution for these issues is fanciful

171
00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:05.040
or experimental treatment. We're simply asking for access to

172
00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:08.960
medicines that are standard of care internationally, medicines that help

173
00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:12.840
people with this unrelenting disease live longer and continue

174
00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.650
contributing to their families and communities.

175
00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:19.719
And yes, we do understand that there is a cost to these modern medicines,

176
00:10:20.279 --> 00:10:22.560
but cost is only one side of the equation.

177
00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.120
The other side is that with modern medicines, myeloma can be a very

178
00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:30.080
livable cancer that doesn't clog up the health system.

179
00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:34.190
Patients continue working, looking after children and grandchildren,

180
00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.020
volunteering, coaching sports teams, living full, productive

181
00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:39.560
lives, and contributing.

182
00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:41.720
You can help make that happen.

183
00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:42.980
Thank you.

184
00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:47.340
Thank you, Barbara. Thank you, Heather.

185
00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:51.180
We've got a minute or two for questions.

186
00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:53.060
Do any members want to ask anything?

187
00:10:57.020 --> 00:11:00.490
It doesn't appear so. Thank you very much, though, for coming in and

188
00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.760
raising more awareness around myeloma

189
00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.340
and blood cancers, and all of the challenges that you guys are

190
00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.800
facing on a personal level, and our sincere

191
00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:13.720
sympathies for that.

192
00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:17.220
Thank you for raising this to the committee. Have a good day.

193
00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:20.220
Thank you for the opportunity. Thank you. Stay warm.

194
00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:22.460
Thank you.

195
00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.640
Okay, we'll move back to private session, please.
